<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/hero-home.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/hero-home-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/hero-home.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="904" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/appraisal-practice/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Homeowners Insurance Appraisal</span></nav><p class="tag">Appraisal service</p><h1>Homeowners Insurance Appraisal Across New Jersey</h1><p class="lede">A Cape Cod in Toms River, a 1920s colonial in Montclair and a two-family in Bayonne don’t cost the same to repair. We value each one on its own construction, not a generic price list.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn amber" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn glass" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Covers</dt><dd>Dwelling, other structures, contents</dd></div><div><dt>Common gap</dt><dd>Scope &amp; matching</dd></div><div><dt>Statewide</dt><dd>All 21 counties</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>The houses behind New Jersey homeowner claims</h2>
<p>New Jersey’s housing stock is old and varied. Northern towns are full of pre-war colonials and Tudors with plaster walls, slate or multi-layer roofs and hardwood floors that can’t be patched invisibly. Postwar suburbs from Middlesex to Camden County are built on ranches, split-levels and bi-levels. Hudson and Passaic counties have thousands of two- and three-family frame houses where one claim touches several units. At the shore, many homes have been raised on pilings since Sandy, and their stairs, decks and utilities sit outside the footprint a desk adjuster sees.</p>
<p>A first estimate often treats all of this as standard drywall and three-tab shingles. That is where most of the underpayments we see begin.</p>
<h2>Gaps we find most often</h2>
<ul>
<li>Plaster-and-lath walls priced as half-inch drywall.</li>
<li>Roof slopes left out because only the visibly damaged side was counted.</li>
<li>Flooring that runs continuously through several rooms, priced as one room.</li>
<li>Uniform Construction Code items, permits and inspections missing from the scope.</li>
<li>Second-unit damage in two-family homes left off a single-unit estimate.</li>
<li>Contents lists depreciated far beyond the items’ real age and condition.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How a homeowner appraisal runs</h2>
<p>We start with a free conversation and a look at your policy, the carrier’s estimate and your photos. If appraisal fits, we inspect, build an independent estimate and, once appraisal is demanded, work it through the panel. If a documented supplement request is more likely to close the gap, we say so. Not every short estimate needs a panel.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Mortgage checks</b>If you have a mortgage, the lender is usually named on claim payments, including any amount set by an appraisal award. Ask your servicer early how it releases funds for repairs.</div>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Do you handle two-family and three-family homes?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="ans"><p>Yes. Owner-occupied multi-family homes are common in North Jersey, and they often need separate scopes for each unit plus shared areas such as roofs, stairs and basements.</p></div></details><details><summary>My house was raised after Sandy. Does that change the claim?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="ans"><p>It can. Elevated homes add stair systems, breakaway walls, pilings and exterior utility runs to the scope. Some of those parts may be covered by flood insurance instead of the homeowners policy, so we separate them carefully.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can you appraise contents as well as the building?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="ans"><p>Yes. Contents disputes usually turn on depreciation and replacement prices. We review the inventory, the depreciation applied and what comparable items cost today.</p></div></details></div><h2>Related services</h2><div class="related"><a class="rel" href="/appraisal-practice/condo-hoa-commercial-appraisal/"><small>Service</small>Condo, HOA &amp; Commercial Appraisal<svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"